  "success": true,
  "output": "Based on the codebase, here are concrete performance findings.\n\n## 1. Unnecessary re-renders — `TranscriptsView`\n\nPrior review found unfiltered lists re-rendering all rows without memoization. Fix pattern:\n\n```tsx\n// Before: every row re-renders on any parent state change\n{transcripts.map(t => <TranscriptRow key={t._id} transcript={t} onSelect={onSelect} />)}\n\n// After\nconst TranscriptRow = React.memo(function TranscriptRow({ transcript, onSelect }: Props) {\n  ...\n});\nconst handleSelect = useCallback((id: string) => onSelect(id), [onSelect]);\n```\n\n## 2. Redundant computation — `buildConversationItems` (`src/utils/transcript.ts`)\n\nCalled on every render of the transcript detail view with `filter`/`map`/`sort` over full arrays each time. Since it's a pure function of `transcript` + `events`, wrap the call site:\n\n```tsx\nconst items = useMemo(() => buildConversationItems(transcript, events), [transcript, events]);\n```\n\n## 3. Campaign strategy objects rebuilt every render\n\nNoted in memory (obs 1395): dialing-strategy objects (`outcome_rules`, `call_windows`, `attempt_sequence`) are reconstructed on each render instead of memoized, which cascades re-renders into child form components that do reference-equality checks.\n\n```tsx\nconst dialingStrategy = useMemo(() => ({\n  ...campaign.dialing_strategy,\n  outcome_rules: campaign.dialing_strategy?.outcome_rules ?? DEFAULT_OUTCOME_RULES,\n}), [campaign.dialing_strategy]);\n```\n\n## 4. `useCountUp` hook — stale closure risk / no cleanup guard\n\n`src/hooks/useCountUp.ts` starts `requestAnimationFrame` and only cancels implicitly by not scheduling further ticks — but if the component unmounts mid-animation, `setValue` still fires on an unmounted component and `ref.current` access is safe only because of optional chaining. Add explicit cancellation:\n\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (target === 0) { setValue(0); return; }\n  let rafId: number;\n  const start = performance.now();\n  ref.current?.classList.add('counting');\n  const tick = (now: number) => {\n    const progress = Math.min((now - start) / duration, 1);\n    const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - progress, 3);\n    setValue(Math.round(target * eased));\n    if (progress < 1) rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    else ref.current?.classList.remove('counting');\n  };\n  rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n  return () => cancelAnimationFrame(rafId); // fixes leak on rapid target changes/unmount\n}, [target, duration]);\n```\nWithout this, rapid target changes (e.g. dashboard counters updating on poll) stack multiple concurrent RAF loops writing to the same `value` state — a real memory/CPU leak under frequent updates.\n\n## 5. `useToasts` — leaked timers\n\n`showToast` schedules `setTimeout(() => dismissToast(id), durationMs)` but never stores/clears the handle. If the component using this hook unmounts before the timeout fires, `setToasts` still runs on a stale closure (harmless here since state is hook-local, but it's a dangling timer that outlives relevance if `showToast` is called rapidly, e.g. in a retry loop):\n\n```tsx\nconst timers = useRef(new Map<string, ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>());\nconst showToast = useCallback((message, tone = 'success', durationMs = DEFAULT_DURATION_MS) => {\n  ...\n  if (durationMs > 0) {\n    const handle = setTimeout(() => { dismissToast(id); timers.current.delete(id); }, durationMs);\n    timers.current.set(id, handle);\n  }\n  return id;\n}, [dismissToast]);\n// on unmount: useEffect(() => () => timers.current.forEach(clearTimeout), []);\n```\n\n## 6. Caching opportunities — `api.ts`\n\n- **No response caching / dedup**: every `api.bots()`, `api.campaigns()`, `api.phrases()` etc. call hits the network fresh even when called from multiple mounted views in the same tick. Add a simple in-flight request dedup + short TTL cache:\n\n```ts\nconst inflight = new Map<string, Promise<unknown>>();\nasync function cachedRequest<T>(path: string, ttlMs = 5000): Promise<T> {\n  const cached = cacheGet<{ ts: number; data: T } | null>(path, null);\n  if (cached && Date.now() - cached.ts < ttlMs) return cached.data;\n  if (inflight.has(path)) return inflight.get(path) as Promise<T>;\n  const p = request<T>(path).then(data => { cacheSet(path, { ts: Date.now(), data }); inflight.delete(path); return data; });\n  inflight.set(path, p);\n  return p;\n}\n```\n- **No HTTP cache headers** (already flagged in memory obs 1394) — static reference data (`OUTCOME_CATEGORIES`, phrase library, language settings) rarely changes; backend should set `Cache-Control` and frontend should respect `304`.\n\n## 7. App.tsx — sequential data-loading effects (obs 1393)\n\nMultiple `useEffect`s firing sequentially post-auth instead of in parallel:\n\n```tsx\n// Before: each effect awaits independently, but if they're chained via shared state they serialize\nuseEffect(() => { api.campaigns().then(setCampaigns); }, []);\nuseEffect(() => { api.transcripts().then(setTranscripts); }, []);\nuseEffect(() => { api.phrases().then(setPhrases); }, []);\n\n// After: fire in parallel explicitly\nuseEffect(() => {\n  Promise.all([api.campaigns(), api.transcripts(), api.phrases(), api.outcomes(), api.languageSettings()])\n    .then(([campaigns, transcripts, phrases, outcomes, languages]) => {\n      setCampaigns(campaigns); setTranscripts(transcripts); setPhrases(phrases);\n      setOutcomes(outcomes); setLanguages(languages);\n    });\n}, []);\n```\nThis isn't N+1 in the DB sense but is the frontend analogue — serialized round trips to the same backend that could be parallelized or (better) served by one combined `/bootstrap` endpoint.\n\n## 8. `OUTCOME_CATEGORIES` / `DEFAULT_OUTCOME_RULES` — fine as-is\n\nThese are module-level constants (`src/constants/outcomes.ts`), already correctly hoisted out of render — no action needed, called out only because they're a good pattern to replicate for `DAY_OPTIONS` and `PHRASE_CATEGORY_META` (already following the same pattern).\n\n## Backend/worker (from prior graph-informed review, still open)\n\n- Callback worker: multiple DB roundtrips per document (obs 1380), sequential doc processing blocking parallelism (obs 1381), payload rebuilt on every retry instead of cached (obs 1391) — these are the actual N+1 patterns in this codebase, but they live in `callback_worker/`, not `frontend/`. Want me to pull the specific line numbers there next?\n\n**Priority order**: #7 (parallel effects) and #6 (request caching) give the biggest perceived-load-time win for least risk; #1/#2 fix real re-render cost in the two heaviest views; #4/#5 are minor leak fixes, safe to batch together.\n",
